course: dictionary
lesson: 14163
title: Making things less bad
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: To mitigate something is to make it less serious, less painful or less harmful. «The sandbags will mitigate the damage when the river floods.» The river will still flood, but the sandbags make the harm smaller. The clue is the word damage: mitigate tells you the damage gets smaller.
explain: You can mitigate a bad feeling too. «Her quick apology helped to mitigate the hurt.» The hurt is still there, but it is not as strong. Mitigate usually sounds formal, so you will see it in news reports, science lessons and school rules.
explain: In law, mitigate has a special meaning. «The judge listened to the mitigating circumstances, such as the boy's young age.» Mitigating circumstances are facts that make a person less to blame, so the punishment may be lighter. Ask yourself: does the word make something worse, or less bad? If it is less bad, mitigate is probably the right idea.
fact: Mitigate means to make something less serious, less painful or less harmful.
fact: «Mitigate the effects» means to reduce how bad the effects are.
fact: «Mitigating circumstances» are facts that make a person less to blame, so a punishment may be lighter.
fact: Mitigate is a verb: mitigate, mitigates, mitigated, mitigating.
fact: Test it by swapping in a simpler word, such as reduce or lessen. If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «The sandbags will mitigate the flood damage.» What does mitigate mean here? | *Make it less harmful | Make it last longer | Make it look pretty | Make it worse
exercise: «The judge thought his young age was a mitigating circumstance.» What are mitigating circumstances? | Money paid to the court | *Facts that make a person less to blame | Things that prove someone is guilty | Rules that make a punishment bigger
exercise: «Planting trees can mitigate climate change.» Which word is closest in meaning to mitigate? | *Reduce | Celebrate | Ignore | Copy
test: «Her kind words helped to mitigate the pain of losing the match.» What does mitigate mean? | Forget | Repeat | Increase | *Lessen
test: Which sentence uses mitigate correctly? | The plaster will mitigate the cut and make it bleed more. | The plaster will mitigate the cut into bigger pieces. | The plaster will mitigate the cut and make it less painful. | *The plaster will mitigate the pain of the cut.
exam: Which phrase is closest to «mitigating factors» in a sentence about a court case? | Facts that make a person more guilty | Facts that prove the date of the trial | *Facts that make a person less to blame | Facts that show the court is closed
